# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""English translations, batch 12 (40 figures)."""
EN = {
"浪子的父亲": dict(lesson="The Father's love always watches from afar",
 summary="The father in the parable who divided the inheritance, waited day after day, and on seeing his repentant younger son from afar ran to embrace him—a picture of the Father's great love receiving sinners.",
 witness="This father is Jesus' portrait of the heavenly Father. He let the younger son leave with his inheritance—there is freedom in love; when the son squandered all and came back in ruin, 'while he was still far off, his father saw him and was moved with compassion, ran and fell on his neck and kissed him.' He did not wait for the son to finish his words of repentance, but called for the best robe, a ring, sandals, and the fattened calf to celebrate: 'this my son was dead and is alive again, was lost and is found.' He also went out to plead with the resentful elder son. The father's love both runs to the returning prodigal and seeks the self-righteous elder son.",
 follow=["Giving people freedom to choose, yet always watching in hope","Moved with compassion and running to meet while the son was still far off","Replacing rebuke with full acceptance in grace","Also going out to seek the self-righteous, bitter elder son"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Like the heavenly Father, open your arms to the one who turns back, not raking up the past","Take the first step toward reconciliation, not standing on ceremony","Rejoice over a prodigal's return rather than counting grievances","Also seek out the resentful, self-righteous"],
 prayer="Heavenly Father, You are like the father who ran to meet, moved with compassion while I was still far off. Make me too receive the one who turns back with such love, take the initiative to reconcile, and rejoice over a life lost and found. Amen."),
"亚多尼雅": dict(lesson="Self-exaltation finally harmed by being exalted",
 summary="A son of David who set himself up as king while his father was old, and after failing also sought to marry Abishag, bringing on himself a death sentence.",
 witness="Adonijah 'exalted himself, saying, I will be king'—he did not wait for God, nor inquire of his father, but arranged chariots and horses and feasted his faction. When Solomon was made king, his scheme failed. He could have lived in peace, but again sought to marry David's concubine Abishag, which at the time was tantamount to coveting the throne, and was finally executed. His life is a model of 'self-exaltation': grasping what was not entrusted to him, not giving up even after being spared once, finally swallowed by his own ambition.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Not waiting for God, grasping the throne and rank on his own","Still not repenting after being spared once, reaching out again","Boosting his own prestige with feasting and faction"],
 applications=["When longing for advancement, first ask whether it is from God and whether the time has come","When spared, treasure it; don't return to the old way","Don't grasp positions and rights not entrusted to you","Let God exalt you, rather than exalting yourself"],
 prayer="Lord, Adonijah exalted himself and was finally harmed by ambition. Make me content with what You measure to me, not grasping what You haven't entrusted, willingly waiting for Your exaltation and time. Amen."),
"伊施波设": dict(lesson="A position propped up by people does not last",
 summary="A son of Saul, set up as king of Israel by the commander Abner, weak and incapable, finally killed in his bed by two captains.",
 witness="Ish-bosheth's throne was given by others from start to finish—Abner set him up, and over one rebuke would abandon him; he 'dared not answer Abner a word,' showing his weakness. He had no anointing from God, only human support; once his supporter turned away, his kingdom collapsed and he died a violent death. He reminds us: a position propped up by connections and power has shallow roots; only what God establishes lasts.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Holding a throne not his own, opposing the one God established","Wholly relying on human support, with no responsibility of his own","Weak when he should be strong, coveting when he should yield"],
 applications=["Don't hold a position God has not given you","The true foundation is God's calling, not human support","In your post, fulfill its duty, not be a puppet","Submit to God's anointed, not opposing God's will"],
 prayer="Lord, Ish-bosheth's throne, propped up by people, finally toppled. Make me not covet a position You haven't given, building my foundation on Your calling, faithful in the duty entrusted to me. Amen."),
"亚玛撒": dict(lesson="One caught amid strife needs extra vigilance",
 summary="The commander during Absalom's rebellion, later pardoned by David and appointed to replace Joab, who after hesitating and failing a task was assassinated by Joab's feigned greeting.",
 witness="Amasa was a man caught in the crevices of power: he first followed Absalom in rebelling against his father, and after failing was shown David's magnanimity, made commander in Joab's place. But when mustering the men of Judah he delayed past the appointed time, giving room for the situation to spin out of control; more fatally, he was utterly unguarded against the murderous Joab—Joab with his right hand 'took Amasa by the beard to kiss him,' the sword in his left hand already in his belly. Amasa reminds us: caught amid strife and power transition, one must both act decisively and stay alert to a hidden knife behind a smile.",
 follow=["Accepting pardon after failure, willing to be used again"],
 caution=["Delaying and bungling a crucial task","Utterly unguarded against one harboring an old grudge"],
 applications=["After being pardoned, respond to what's entrusted with faithfulness and decisiveness","Be extra vigilant in the sensitive setting of power transition","Be wary of those who greet you with a smile but harbor enmity","Finish what's entrusted on time, giving no room to chaos"],
 prayer="Lord, Amasa, amid strife, hesitated and was off guard, and lost his life. Give me a decisive, dutiful heart, and discernment and vigilance, to stand firm in You in complex situations. Amen."),
"革罗罢": dict(lesson="The Lord often walks with us on the road of disappointment",
 summary="One of the two disciples on the road to Emmaus who, walking home in disappointment, journeyed with the risen Lord without recognizing Him, until their eyes were opened at the breaking of bread.",
 witness="On the third day after the passion, Cleopas and his companion walked dejected toward Emmaus: 'We had hoped that He was the one to redeem Israel.' Hope was shattered. Yet the risen Jesus drew near and walked with them, explaining the Scriptures from Moses and all the prophets, and though their 'hearts burned' they still did not recognize Him—until the moment of breaking bread at the table, when their eyes were opened, they recognized the Lord, and He vanished. They at once rose and hurried back to Jerusalem to tell the news. The Lord often quietly walks with us on our most disappointing road, revealing Himself through His word and the breaking of bread.",
 follow=["Still talking together about the Lord amid disappointment","Rising at once to proclaim when the Lord opened their eyes"],
 caution=["Once unable, because of present disappointment, to see the Lord right beside them"],
 applications=["When disappointed and downcast, remember the Lord may be walking with you","Let God's word 'explaining the Scriptures' kindle your cooled heart","Experience the Lord's presence in the breaking of bread/fellowship","Once you recognize the Lord, rise at once to share with others"],
 prayer="Risen Lord, You walked with the disappointed disciples on the Emmaus road, opening their eyes through Scripture and the breaking of bread. Walk with me too on my discouraged road, kindle my heart, that I may recognize You and proclaim You. Amen."),
"哈拿尼雅（假先知）": dict(lesson="A pleasant false prophecy cannot stop real judgment",
 summary="A false prophet who opposed Jeremiah, broke the wooden yoke off Jeremiah's neck, declared that within two years the exiled vessels would return, and died that very year.",
 witness="When Jeremiah warned the people with a wooden yoke that they must serve Babylon, the false prophet Hananiah broke the yoke publicly, declaring God would break Babylon's yoke 'within two years.' This is what everyone loves to hear—fast, cheap, pleasing all. But it was a lie. Jeremiah replied: you have broken a wooden yoke, but in its place you get an iron yoke. God declared through Jeremiah: 'You have made this people trust in a lie... this year you shall die.' In the seventh month of that year, Hananiah died. A people-pleasing false prophecy cannot change God's word; it only kills the one who speaks it and those who believe it.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Preaching a 'peace' message people love to hear but not from God","Packaging a lie with dramatic action","Resisting God's true warning with a quick-fix promise"],
 applications=["Discern whether a message is from God, not whether it's pleasant","Beware voices that only say 'peace, peace'","Rather hear a jarring truth than believe a pleasing lie","Don't package your own wishes in spiritual language"],
 prayer="Lord, Hananiah resisted Your word with a pleasant false prophecy and brought destruction on himself. Give me a discerning heart, willing to receive jarring truth rather than be deceived by a flattering lie. Amen."),
"巴施户珥": dict(lesson="Persecuting the messenger cannot change the message",
 summary="The chief officer of the temple who struck Jeremiah and put him in the stocks; Jeremiah declared he and all his household would be exiled to Babylon and die there.",
 witness="Pashhur was a priest with authority in the temple. Hearing Jeremiah's prophecy, instead of examining himself, he acted—striking the prophet and putting him in the stocks, to shame and suppress this jarring voice. But at daybreak the stocks were opened, and God's word grew heavier instead: Jeremiah renamed him 'Magor-missabib' (terror on every side), declaring he would see his friends fall by the sword with his own eyes and himself be exiled to die in Babylon. Persecuting the messenger cannot in the least stop God's appointed word—it only brings the judgment on oneself.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Using power to suppress and persecute the one who speaks truth","Facing rebuke, choosing to strike rather than examine himself","Thinking that removing the messenger removes the message"],
 applications=["When you hear jarring words, examine yourself first rather than strike back","Don't use authority to crush those who point out problems","Remember suppressing truth only adds to your own responsibility","Respect and protect those faithfully conveying God's word"],
 prayer="Lord, Pashhur persecuted the prophet, yet could not stop Your word and brought judgment instead. Make me, facing rebuke, humbly examine myself, not suppress truth with power, but respect the one You send. Amen."),
"押撒": dict(lesson="The one who inherits also asks for the spring of living water",
 summary="Caleb's daughter, Othniel's wife; having received the Negev as an inheritance, she further asked her father for springs of water, and received the upper and lower springs.",
 witness="Achsah was a woman of faith who dared to ask. Her father Caleb gave her in marriage to Othniel, who captured Kiriath-sepher, and gave her the Negev as inheritance. But the Negev was dry, and she did not stop at 'having land'; she got off her donkey and asked her father: 'Give me also springs of water.' And Caleb gave her the upper and lower springs. Achsah reminds us: God's inheritance sometimes still lacks 'springs of water'—we can boldly ask the heavenly Father for the living water that gives the inheritance life, and He gladly gives richly.",
 follow=["Treasuring the inheritance received, and asking for the springs that give it life","Boldly asking her father (a figure of the heavenly Father)","Having faith and vision, not stopping at the status quo"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Having received God's gift, also ask for the living water that waters it","Boldly and fearlessly ask the heavenly Father for good things","Ask for heavenly watering over what you inherit (family, work, gifts)","Not content with 'having,' pursue 'having life'"],
 prayer="Heavenly Father, Achsah, having received the inheritance, still asked for springs, and Your fatherly heart gladly gave richly. Give me living water to water all You give, that it not dry up but bear the fruit of life. Amen."),
"约亚拿": dict(lesson="Supplying the Lord's work with all you have",
 summary="The wife of Chuza, Herod's steward, who with several women supplied Jesus and the disciples from her own means, and was also one of the witnesses of the empty tomb.",
 witness="Joanna was of no low standing—her husband was Herod's steward. Yet she willingly followed this teacher from Nazareth, 'providing for' Jesus and the disciples' needs from her own means. Beyond the male disciples, it was this group of women who quietly carried the supply of the Lord's team. In the end, when the disciples scattered, she was among those who came to the tomb early, first heard the resurrection news, and went back to report to the apostles. Joanna reminds us: supplying God's work with the resources you have is precious service, remembered by God.",
 follow=["Willingly supplying the Lord's work from her own means","Following the Lord humbly across her status","Faithful to the end, becoming a witness of the resurrection"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Supply God's ministry with your money and resources","Don't be ashamed to follow the Lord because of status","Value supply-type service behind the scenes","Follow faithfully to the end, and you too can be a witness"],
 prayer="Lord, Joanna supplied Your work from all she had, and faithfully witnessed the resurrection. Make me too willingly offer resources to serve You, faithful behind the scenes, until I witness Your glory. Amen."),
"亚那": dict(lesson="Self-interest cloaked in holy office is most dangerous",
 summary="A former high priest, Caiaphas' father-in-law, who though retired still held the power of the priestly family, and privately tried Jesus at night.",
 witness="Annas was the true power-holder of the priestly clique of that day—five of his sons and his son-in-law Caiaphas served as high priest in succession, and the temple trade's interests were deeply entangled. When Jesus was arrested, He was first brought before Annas for a private trial, which itself was illegal. He wore the most sacred office, yet within was maintaining the family's power and vested interests. Annas reminds us: the most dangerous corruption is not open evil, but self-interest and scheming wrapped in holy office, in a religious cloak.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Using a sacred office to shield family interests","Manipulating in secret, conducting an unjust trial","Suppressing truth with religious authority"],
 applications=["Beware self-interest and power packaged in a spiritual identity","Don't let vested interests distort your attitude toward truth","Hold justice even in secret, no backroom dealings","Often examine: am I serving God, or my own interest?"],
 prayer="Lord, Annas wore holy office yet harbored self-interest, secretly trying Your beloved Son unjustly. Cleanse my motives in service, that I not use a spiritual identity for private gain, but hold justice in secret. Amen."),
"尼尼微王": dict(lesson="True repentance begins with coming down from the throne and bowing",
 summary="The king reigning when Jonah proclaimed judgment to Nineveh, who came down from his throne, put on sackcloth and ashes, led the whole city in fasting and repentance, and God relented from the disaster He had spoken.",
 witness="A king of a great pagan city, hearing a foreign prophet's one sentence 'forty days and Nineveh will be overthrown,' actually 'rose from his throne, removed his robe, put on sackcloth, and sat in ashes,' and decreed the whole city, even the animals, to fast and each turn from his evil way: 'Who knows? God may relent... so that we do not perish.' This is one of the most thorough national repentances in Scripture. And God 'saw their deeds... and did not bring on them the disaster He had threatened.' The king of Nineveh reminds us: true repentance begins with the humble bowing of the highest, and God's mercy is always open to those who truly turn back.",
 follow=["On hearing the warning, responding humbly at once, not relying on royal power","Leading by example, repenting starting with himself","Leading the whole city to turn from evil, seeking God's mercy"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["When you hear God's warning, respond at once, not delay","The higher your position, the more you should lead in humble repentance","True repentance is 'turning from the evil way,' not only a ritual","Trust God's mercy is open to those who truly turn back"],
 prayer="Lord, the king of Nineveh, hearing the warning, came down from his throne, put on sackcloth, and repented, and You showed mercy. Give me the same undelaying repentance not relying on status, leading those I influence to turn to You together. Amen."),
"哈拿尼（先见）": dict(lesson="Faithful words, though jarring, must still be spoken",
 summary="The seer who rebuked King Asa for relying on Aram and not on God in his later years, and was put in prison for his frankness.",
 witness="Hanani was a seer who rebuked Asa for relying on the king of Aram rather than on God in his later years, and was put in prison for it. He would rather sit in prison than fail to speak God's word as it was. Faithful words are often jarring, but a faithful servant does not rewrite God's message to suit people's faces.",
 follow=["Speaking God's word as it is, not minding people's faces","Prepared to pay a price for speaking the truth"],
 caution=["Don't fail to speak frankly just because the person has merit or power"],
 applications=["Don't hide a truth that should be spoken out of personal consideration","Remind people (and yourself) the root question is 'whom do you rely on'","Be prepared to bear the cost of speaking truth"],
 prayer="Lord, Hanani spoke truth at the risk of prison. Give me courage to speak faithfully and frankly, not hiding Your word for the sake of people's faces and power. Amen."),
"耶弗他的女儿": dict(lesson="In another's rashness, still respond with obedience",
 summary="When Jephthah returned victorious, his daughter came out first to meet him, but because of her father's rash vow she was offered up, obediently accepting this fate.",
 witness="Before battle Jephthah rashly vowed: on returning victorious, the first to come out his door to meet him would be offered to the LORD. He won, and the one who came out was his only daughter, beating a tambourine and dancing to meet her father. This was a tragedy bred by a father's recklessness. Yet the daughter's response is moving: 'My father, you have opened your mouth to the LORD; do to me according to what has gone out of your mouth.' She asked only for two months to weep with her companions. In her father's rashness and that dark age, she faced it with astonishing obedience and dignity. Her story both warns against rash vows and reveals the nobility of a young life.",
 follow=["Responding to an unjust fate with obedience and dignity","Honoring the vow made, valuing a promise to God"],
 caution=["(A warning against her father) a rash vow that implicated an innocent loved one"],
 applications=["Don't vow rashly to God, lest you implicate others","Think before you speak and promise; keep your word","Keep dignity and faith amid a plight caused by others","Remember those who quietly bore suffering in dark ages"],
 prayer="Lord, Jephthah's daughter responded with obedience amid her father's rashness. Make me careful in speech and not rash in vows, and give me a heart that keeps dignity and trust amid unjust situations. Amen."),
"浪子": dict(lesson="The moment he came to himself and went home, the father was waiting all along",
 summary="The younger son in the parable who asked for his inheritance, went to a far country and squandered it all, fell to feeding pigs, then came to himself and went home, fully received by his father.",
 witness="He demanded his inheritance early—as if to say 'I wish you were dead'; then went far, lived recklessly, and spent everything, until he was among the pigs with no one even giving him pods. At the lowest point he 'came to himself': even my father's hired servants have plenty, yet I am dying here of hunger! He rose and went home with a prepared speech of repentance. While still far off, his father ran and embraced him. Before he could finish his words of repentance, the robe, ring, sandals, and fattened calf all came. The prodigal reminds us: however far we've gone, however hard we've fallen, if we come to ourselves and turn back, the father is waiting at the road's edge, to receive us fully.",
 follow=["At the lowest point 'coming to himself,' admitting his sin","Rising and truly returning to the father","Confessing frankly: 'I have sinned against heaven and before you'"],
 caution=["Self-centered, squandering all God gave","Only turning back after hitting bottom"],
 applications=["However hard you've fallen, it's never too late to rise and go home","Truly confess your sin to God, making no excuses","Trust the heavenly Father waits at the road's edge to receive you fully","Help those 'in the far country' come to themselves and go home"],
 prayer="Heavenly Father, when the prodigal came to himself and went home, You were already running to meet him at the road's edge. Make all of us who have gone far willing to rise and go home, confess our sin frankly, and know Your acceptance is wholly unconditional. Amen."),
"浪子的哥哥": dict(lesson="Staying home, yet the heart can be far from the father",
 summary="The elder son in the parable who, though law-abiding, was angry at the father throwing a feast for his returned brother and refused to go in.",
 witness="The elder son never left home, labored for years, and never disobeyed his father's command. Yet when his brother returned and the father held a feast, he 'was angry and refused to go in.' He said to his father: I have served you these many years, and you never gave me a young goat... 'but when this son of yours came,' you killed the fattened calf for him! He kept the rules, but his heart was full of comparison, self-righteousness, and bitterness—at home, but his heart far from the father's heart. The father came out and pleaded: 'You are always with me, and all that is mine is yours.' The elder son warns us: the law-abiding too can be unable, through self-righteousness, to share the father's joy.",
 follow=["Years of obedience, keeping his duty"],
 caution=["Comparing self-righteously, looking down on the one who turned back","Resentful that grace came to another","At home in the father's house, yet full of bitterness, refusing to go in"],
 applications=["Beware the self-righteousness and comparison behind 'keeping the rules'","Truly rejoice when others are shown grace and a prodigal returns","Enjoy the blessing of being with the Father, not just eyeing the 'reward'","Let the father's heart be your heart: rejoice over the lost found"],
 prayer="Heavenly Father, the prodigal's elder brother stayed home, yet his heart was far from You through self-righteousness. Heal the comparison and bitterness in me, that I rejoice over others being shown grace and truly enjoy the blessing of being with You. Amen."),
"悔改的强盗": dict(lesson="Last-moment faith is also remembered by the Lord",
 summary="One of the robbers crucified with Jesus, who in his final hour repented, asked the Lord to remember him, and was promised that very day to enter paradise together.",
 witness="Both robbers were crucified with Jesus. One mocked, but the other, in the last few hours of life, saw the truth: he rebuked his companion, 'Do you not fear God, since you are under the same sentence?' admitting he was 'justly' punished, and recognizing the innocent dying Jesus as King: 'Jesus, remember me when You come into Your kingdom!' He had not been baptized, done no good works, had no time to make amends—only a faith turning to Christ. The Lord at once promised: 'Today you will be with Me in paradise.' He is the purest witness of grace: salvation by grace through faith, unrelated to merit—even at the last moment.",
 follow=["Still willing to repent and fear God at the brink of death","Frankly admitting his sin, recognizing Christ as King","Entrusting himself wholly to the Lord's mercy"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["It's never too late to turn back, but don't put it off to the end","Salvation depends on trusting Christ, not accumulating merit","Frankly admit your sin, asking only that the Lord remember you","Proclaim this receiving Lord to the dying and desperate"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, the robber on the cross turned to You at the last moment and was promised entry into paradise. Thank You that Your grace is so great; make me trust only Your mercy, and bring this hope to the desperate. Amen."),
"财主（与拉撒路）": dict(lesson="This life's revelry cannot bridge the eternal chasm",
 summary="The rich man in the parable who feasted lavishly every day yet ignored Lazarus the beggar at his gate, and after death suffered torment in Hades.",
 witness="The rich man 'was clothed in purple and fine linen and feasted sumptuously every day,' while Lazarus, covered with sores, lay at his gate longing for the scraps from his table. The rich man did not do some great evil—he 'ignored' the suffering right at his gate. After death the situations were reversed: Lazarus in Abraham's bosom, the rich man in the flames of Hades, with 'a great chasm fixed' between, impossible to cross. He begged to send Lazarus to warn his five brothers, and the answer was: they have Moses and the prophets; 'if they do not hear them, neither will they be convinced if someone rises from the dead.' The rich man warns: ignoring the poor near you and making this life's revelry everything brings an irreversible eternity.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Feasting every day, ignoring the suffering at his gate","Self-satisfied in wealth, ignoring God's word","Only in eternity realizing all is beyond repair"],
 applications=["Open your eyes to the need at your 'gate' and reach out to help","Don't let wealth numb you to others' pain","While in this life, heed God's word and earnestly prepare for eternity","Use this life's wealth to make eternal friends"],
 prayer="Lord, the rich man, ignoring Lazarus at his gate, regretted it forever in eternity. Open my eyes to the need near me, that I not be deceived by wealth, but heed Your word in this life and earnestly prepare for eternity. Amen."),
"不义的管家": dict(lesson="If worldly people know to plan ahead, God's people should plan for eternity",
 summary="The steward in the parable who, about to be dismissed, cleverly prepared a way out for himself; the master praised his shrewdness, and the Lord used it to teach the disciples to use money well.",
 witness="This is an easily misread parable. The steward, about to be dismissed for wasting his master's goods, while he still had authority reduced the debtors' debts, preparing a way out so that 'people would receive him into their houses.' The master 'commended the dishonest steward for his shrewdness'—commending not his dishonesty, but his foresight and decisiveness. From this the Lord taught: 'the sons of this world... are more shrewd than the sons of light.' If even worldly people know to plan for the future, how much more should God's people use this life's money ('unrighteous wealth') to make eternal friends? It is a lesson on shrewdness, foresight, and using resources well.",
 follow=["Having foresight, knowing to plan ahead for the future","Acting decisively within the only window of opportunity"],
 caution=["His means themselves were dishonest; the Lord praised the shrewdness, not the deceit","If shrewdness is used for selfishness, it becomes a warning"],
 applications=["Plan for eternity as worldly people plan for the future","Use this life's money for things of eternal value","While you still have opportunity and authority, do good decisively","Use resources to make eternal friends"],
 prayer="Lord, even the dishonest steward knew to plan for the future. Give me heavenly shrewdness and foresight, to use all this life's resources well for things of eternal value, making eternal friends. Amen."),
"撒非喇": dict(lesson="Be of one heart to do good together, not to conspire to deceive",
 summary="The wife of Ananias, who conspired with her husband to sell a field, secretly keep back part of the price while claiming to have given it all, lying to the Holy Spirit, and fell dead on the spot.",
 witness="The early church was full of the beauty of generous sharing, but Ananias and Sapphira wanted the good name without paying the true price: they sold a field, secretly kept back some, yet claimed to give it all. The problem was not that they kept money (Peter said: after it was sold, wasn't the proceeds at your disposal?), but that they 'lied to the Holy Spirit,' trading a lie for spiritual approval. Three hours later Sapphira came in, Peter gave her a chance to tell the truth, but she went along with the lie, and at once fell dead. This couple warns: husband and wife should be of one heart to do good, not to conspire in fraud; falsehood before God is far more terrible than poverty before people.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Husband and wife of one heart—yet conspiring to deceive","Lying to God to win a good name","Insisting on the lie even when given a chance to tell the truth"],
 applications=["Be honest in giving and service, doing what you can, not chasing vanity","Husband and wife should do good of one heart, reminding each other onto the right path","There must not be the least falsehood before God","Give people (and accept) a chance to tell the truth, turning back in time"],
 prayer="Lord, Sapphira conspired with her husband to lie to the Holy Spirit and brought destruction on herself. Make me wholly honest before You, not chasing vanity; and make me do good of one heart with my loved ones, not conspire in fraud. Amen."),
"士求保罗": dict(lesson="A man of understanding hungers for the truth",
 summary="The proconsul of Cyprus, a man of understanding, who hungered to hear God's word; when the magician Elymas opposed, Paul rebuked him to blindness, and the proconsul believed.",
 witness="Sergius Paulus was a Roman-appointed proconsul—a man of position and discernment, 'a man of understanding.' He actively sent for Barnabas and Saul, 'wanting to hear the word of God.' The magician Elymas (Bar-Jesus) beside him strongly opposed, to turn the proconsul from the faith. Paul, filled with the Spirit, fixed his eyes and rebuked this 'son of the devil,' striking him temporarily blind. The proconsul, seeing what happened, was 'astonished at the teaching of the Lord' and believed. Sergius Paulus reminds us: those with position and learning also need, and can, hunger for truth; and for the one who truly seeks, God will clear away the hindrance.",
 follow=["In high position still actively hungering for and seeking God's word","Not self-satisfied in status and learning, willing to listen","Believing readily on seeing God's work"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Whatever your status or learning, humbly seek truth","Arrange opportunities to hear and seek the truth for yourself","Don't be stopped by voices around you that oppose truth","Respond in faith on seeing God's work"],
 prayer="Lord, the proconsul Sergius Paulus hungered for Your word with an understanding heart, and believed despite hindrance. Give me a humble, seeking heart, not self-satisfied in learning and status, and clear away hindrances for all who truly seek You. Amen."),
"基土拉": dict(lesson="Beyond the promise, God also watches over",
 summary="Abraham's later wife, who bore him Midian and five other sons.",
 witness="Keturah was Abraham's wife in his old age, who bore him six sons including Midian. Though these sons were not in the line of the promised inheritance (Abraham sent them away, the inheritance going to Isaac), God still made them into nations. God's care also reaches those outside the main line of the promise.",
 follow=["Raising and nurturing children in your own place"],
 caution=["Accepting God's appointed order (the inheritance to Isaac), not striving"],
 applications=["Accept God's different arrangements for different people","Be dutiful in your own place, not envying others' 'promise'","Trust God's care also reaches those 'outside the main line'"],
 prayer="Lord, though Keturah's descendants did not inherit the promise, You still watched over them. Make me content with the place You appoint, not envious, not striving, trusting Your complete care. Amen."),
"米甸": dict(lesson="Of the same source, yet taking different roads",
 summary="A son of Abraham and Keturah, ancestor of the Midianites.",
 witness="Midian was a son of Abraham and Keturah, ancestor of the Midianites. The Midianites were blood relatives of Israel (both from Abraham), yet later repeatedly became Israel's enemies. Yet Moses' father-in-law Jethro was also a Midianite priest—within the same people, there were both opposers and those who feared God.",
 follow=["Imitate those in Midian like Jethro who feared God"],
 caution=["Blood relatives too can take the road of opposing God's people"],
 applications=["Don't judge a person's or people's spiritual state by blood","Be the one who fears God in your own group","Pray for relatives estranged from or opposing God's people"],
 prayer="Lord, Midian and Israel were of the same source yet parted ways. Make me, whatever people I come from, one who fears You, and have mercy on all relatives far from You. Amen."),
"拉班": dict(lesson="Shrewd in calculation, yet cannot outwit God's care",
 summary="Brother of Rebekah, father of Leah and Rachel, Jacob's father-in-law.",
 witness="Laban was Rebekah's brother, father of Leah and Rachel, Jacob's father-in-law. Shrewd and worldly, he changed Jacob's wages ten times, taking advantage at every turn; yet God watched over Jacob all the way, making him prosper even amid exploitation. The one who calculates against people cannot, in the end, outwit the God who watches over.",
 follow=["Having care and concern even for his family"],
 caution=["Treating people with calculation, fickle, taking advantage","Measuring relationships by gain, regardless of fairness"],
 applications=["Treat people (especially family and employees) fairly, not calculating advantage","When mistreated, trust God's care and recompense","Don't let gain corrode kinship and trust"],
 prayer="Lord, Laban calculated against Jacob, yet You watched over and kept him. Remove from me the habit of calculating for advantage, make me treat people fairly; and when mistreated, let me firmly trust You will watch over me. Amen."),
"辟拉": dict(lesson="A maid's status, yet woven into God's family",
 summary="Rachel's maid, who bore Dan and Naphtali.",
 witness="Bilhah was Rachel's maid, later Jacob's concubine, who bore Dan and Naphtali. As a maid, of lowly status, she became the mother of two tribes of Israel. In God's genealogy there is room for a lowly maid—origin cannot hinder God's use.",
 follow=["Doing her duty in a lowly status","Accepting God's arrangement in her life"],
 caution=["(Background) the family strife stirred to obtain children is worth reflecting on"],
 applications=["Whatever your status, be faithful in God's arrangement","Don't judge your own or others' worth by origin or status","Trust God can use the lowliest to accomplish great things"],
 prayer="Lord, even the lowly maid Bilhah became the mother of tribes. Make me not despair because of lowly status, but be faithful in the place You appoint, knowing what You value is not origin. Amen."),
"悉帕": dict(lesson="Given back and forth, yet God remembers",
 summary="Leah's maid, who bore Gad and Asher.",
 witness="Zilpah was Leah's maid, later Jacob's concubine, who bore Gad and Asher. She was given back and forth by her mistress as a tool for bearing children, with no say of her own, yet God remembered her, making her the mother of two tribes. In a situation beyond her control, God still watched over and still used her.",
 follow=["Still remembered by God in a situation beyond her control"],
 caution=["(Background) the family rivalry treating a person as a tool for childbearing is a warning"],
 applications=["In situations beyond your control, still trust God's care","Don't treat people as tools to achieve ends","Trust God remembers every lowly, overlooked person"],
 prayer="Lord, Zilpah had no say, yet You remembered and used her. Make me trust Your care in situations beyond my control, and remind me not to treat people as tools. Amen."),
"底拿": dict(lesson="One casual outing, drawn into a storm",
 summary="Daughter of Jacob and Leah, the central figure in the Shechem incident.",
 witness="Dinah was the daughter of Jacob and Leah. She went out to see the women of the land, but was violated by Shechem, which then triggered her brothers' bloody revenge, plunging the whole family into crisis. A young woman's experience became the epitome of a victim in that violent age.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["(A warning to victims) the danger of lacking care and caution in an unfamiliar environment"],
 applications=["Stay alert in unfamiliar or unsafe environments","Intercede for those who have been violated, offering protection rather than judgment","Remember God watches over the hurt and the wronged"],
 prayer="Lord, Dinah was hurt in a storm. Protect the weak and helpless, comfort every violated heart, and make me one who protects people, not one who judges. Amen."),
"示剑": dict(lesson="Indulging lust harms others and oneself",
 summary="Son of Hamor the Hivite, who violated Dinah.",
 witness="Shechem was the son of Hamor the Hivite, who out of lust violated Dinah. Even though afterward he was 'drawn to Dinah' and willing to pay any bride-price, he could not undo the harm already done, and finally perished with the whole city in Simeon and Levi's revenge. A moment's indulged lust ruined the other, and ruined himself and the whole city.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Indulging lust, satisfying selfish desire by force","Thinking later amends could erase the harm done"],
 applications=["Restrain lust, respecting others' inviolable dignity","Understand some harm, once done, cannot be undone, so guard yourself beforehand","Before desire, choose the fear of God over indulgence"],
 prayer="Lord, Shechem indulged lust, harming others and himself. Cleanse my desires, make me respect people and fear You, guarding myself before temptation, leaving no harm that cannot be repaired. Amen."),
"哈抹": dict(lesson="Indulgent love brews great disaster",
 summary="A Hivite, father of Shechem.",
 witness="Hamor was a Hivite, father of Shechem. His son violated Dinah, yet he went to Jacob's household to propose marriage and smooth things over, hoping to resolve it by intermarriage. As a result Simeon and Levi by a ruse killed him and every male of the city. Indulging his son's offense and trying to cover it by compromise instead brought the disaster of annihilation.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Indulging a child's offense, trying to cover it by compromise","Avoiding the sin and responsibility he should face, to keep the peace"],
 applications=["Don't indulge or cover a child's (or your own) offense","Address sin head-on, rather than gloss over it by compromise","Let discipline and love go together, lest a greater disaster brew"],
 prayer="Lord, Hamor indulged and covered, and brought great disaster instead. Make me not indulge or cover sin, but address offenses head-on with love and justice, lest a greater calamity grow. Amen."),
"谢拉（犹大之子）": dict(lesson="Born of the same mother, yet different destinies",
 summary="One of the twins born to Judah and Tamar.",
 witness="Zerah was one of the twins born to Judah and Tamar, born at the same time as Perez. At the birth his hand came out first and a scarlet thread was tied on it, yet Perez 'broke out first' and gained the firstborn's status and the Messianic line. God's choice is often unexpected, not according to the order people set.",
 follow=["Accepting God's sovereign choice and order"],
 caution=["Not harboring resentment over 'I should have been first'"],
 applications=["Accept the unexpected arrangements in God's sovereignty","Don't be jealous and resentful when another 'breaks out first'","Serve God in peace in your own place"],
 prayer="Lord, Zerah should have come first, but the choice fell on Perez. Make me submit to Your sovereign arrangement, not jealous or resentful, at peace in the place You appoint. Amen."),
"波提乏": dict(lesson="Using talent well, yet also believing slander",
 summary="Captain of Pharaoh's guard, Joseph's master.",
 witness="Potiphar was the captain of Pharaoh's guard, Joseph's master. He saw the LORD was with Joseph and put everything in his house under Joseph's care, gaining great blessing; yet at his wife's false accusation, without investigation, he put Joseph in prison. His insight into people lost to a moment's one-sided listening.",
 follow=["Valuing and using the faithful and reliable one with God's presence"],
 caution=["Not investigating when listening to one side","Wrongly judging a faithful servant because of a family relationship"],
 applications=["Use the faithful and reliable, entrusting responsibility","When an accusation comes, investigate first, not listening to one side","Don't wrong the innocent because of closeness of relationship"],
 prayer="Lord, Potiphar knew Joseph's faithfulness, yet wronged him by listening to slander. Make me use the faithful, and judge fairly with investigation, not wronging the innocent. Amen."),
"波提乏的妻子": dict(lesson="Lust unfulfilled turns into framing",
 summary="The woman who framed Joseph and tempted him.",
 witness="Potiphar's wife enticed Joseph day after day, and Joseph firmly refused: 'How then can I do this great wickedness and sin against God?' Enraged and shamed, she grabbed Joseph's garment and falsely accused him, sending him to prison. Lust, once refused and turned to revenge, is exceedingly vicious.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Indulging lust, enticing and pestering others","After lust is refused, taking revenge by false accusation"],
 applications=["Restrain your own lust, not enticing or pestering","When refused, don't take revenge in resentment","Imitate Joseph: facing enticement, flee decisively and fear God"],
 prayer="Lord, Potiphar's wife harmed Joseph with lust and false accusation, yet You exalted Joseph. Cleanse my heart, that I not indulge lust or take revenge in resentment, but fear You before enticement like Joseph. Amen."),
"酒政": dict(lesson="Forgetting kindness when prosperous; vigilance is needed",
 summary="Pharaoh's cupbearer, for whom Joseph interpreted a dream.",
 witness="The cupbearer was Pharaoh's officer, imprisoned for offending the king, and Joseph interpreted his dream, saying he would be restored in three days. Joseph asked him to remember him when released, but after restoration he forgot Joseph for a full two years, until Pharaoh had a dream and he remembered. Once prosperous, he forgot the one who helped him in trouble.",
 follow=["Restoration was a turning point given by God"],
 caution=["Forgetting, when prosperous, the one who helped him in trouble","Delaying the duty owed—slow to give thanks and repay"],
 applications=["Remember those who helped you in your lows, and repay in time","Don't forget your friends in adversity when in good times","Don't delay the good you should do and the kindness you should show"],
 prayer="Lord, the cupbearer forgot Joseph when prosperous. Make me not forget those who helped me in trouble, ever grateful in good times, repaying in time and not delaying to do good. Amen."),
"膳长": dict(lesson="The same situation, a different end",
 summary="Pharaoh's chief baker, for whom Joseph interpreted a dream.",
 witness="The chief baker was imprisoned with the cupbearer, and Joseph interpreted his dream too, but it was an omen of doom: in three days he would be hanged on a tree. After three days it came true. In the same prison, both having dreams interpreted, the ends were poles apart—God's word is true, judgment and rescue each by its reality.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["(Background) the reality of sin and judgment, not to be taken lightly"],
 applications=["Fear the truth of God's word; judgment and rescue both surely come","While you still have opportunity, seriously face your relationship with God","Don't gamble on luck; take seriously the end of your life"],
 prayer="Lord, the baker's end fulfilled what You spoke through Joseph. Make me fear the truth of Your word, seriously face the end of life, and harbor no false hope. Amen."),
"提幔人以利法": dict(lesson="Orthodox doctrine, applied to the wrong person",
 summary="One of Job's three friends, who judged Job by traditional wisdom.",
 witness="Eliphaz the Temanite was the foremost of Job's three friends, urging Job to confess his sin by the traditional wisdom 'good rewarded with good, evil with evil.' His words were not without reason, but applied in the wrong place—Job in his suffering was not being punished for sin. God later rebuked them: 'you have not spoken of Me what is right, as My servant Job has.'",
 follow=["Having the initial intent to comfort the suffering (the first seven days of silent companionship)"],
 caution=["Judging another's suffering by a rigid cause-and-effect","Right doctrine with wrong application"],
 applications=["When accompanying the suffering, listen first rather than rush to judge","Don't condemn the suffering with 'you must have sinned to suffer'","Be careful: even right doctrine can be applied to the wrong person"],
 prayer="Lord, Eliphaz judged Job's suffering with right doctrine. Make me, when accompanying the suffering, first have compassion and be careful in judging, lest I wound people with truth. Amen."),
"书亚人比勒达": dict(lesson="Relying on tradition, yet falling into cold harshness",
 summary="One of Job's three friends.",
 witness="Bildad the Shuhite was one of Job's three friends, who brought out 'the tradition of the ancients' to urge Job, even coldly implying Job's children died because of sin. He held to the framework of tradition, but lacked compassion at his friend's most painful moment. Right tradition without mercy becomes piercing words.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Coldly judging the suffering with traditional dogma","Lacking compassion at a person's most painful time"],
 applications=["When speaking to the suffering, have compassion before reasoning","Don't bring out dogma to explain another's pain","Let truth be spoken wrapped in mercy"],
 prayer="Lord, Bildad wounded Job with cold tradition. Make me have more compassion and less dogma toward the suffering, proclaiming truth with mercy. Amen."),
"拿玛人琐法": dict(lesson="Fierce words, even harsher judgment",
 summary="One of Job's three friends.",
 witness="Zophar the Naamathite was the fiercest in words of Job's three friends, asserting that Job's suffering was 'less than your guilt deserves,' urging him to confess quickly. Full of self-confidence, he spoke for God, yet misunderstood God's heart, and finally with the other two needed Job to offer sacrifice and intercede for them.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Confidently speaking for God, in fact misunderstanding His heart","Pressing and condemning the suffering with fierce words"],
 applications=["Don't lightly pronounce 'on God's behalf' to condemn others' sin","Speak to the suffering with restraint, not fierce pressing","Admit you too can misunderstand, holding a humble, teachable heart"],
 prayer="Lord, Zophar fiercely spoke for You wrongly. Make me speak with restraint and humility, not presumptuously condemning people's sin on Your behalf, treating the suffering with mercy and truth. Amen."),
"暗兰": dict(lesson="Ordinary parents raising extraordinary children",
 summary="Grandson of Levi, father of Moses, Aaron, and Miriam.",
 witness="Amram was a descendant of Levi, the father of Moses, Aaron, and Miriam. Scripture records little of him, but it was precisely his and Jochebed's Levite home that, amid the terror of slavery and infanticide, raised Moses and Aaron who led Israel out of Egypt. God's salvation began with a God-fearing home.",
 follow=["Still fearing God and raising children in a hard, terrifying environment","Being the foundation for the next generation's calling"],
 caution=["Not letting the terror of circumstances rob you of faith in raising children"],
 applications=["Wholeheartedly raise the next generation even in hard situations","Trust God can raise extraordinary vessels from an ordinary home","Entrust your children's future to God"],
 prayer="Lord, Amram's household raised Moses and Aaron amid slavery. Make me, in hardship, still fear You and faithfully raise children, knowing You can raise salvation from an ordinary home. Amen."),
"法老的女儿": dict(lesson="One thought of compassion rewrites history",
 summary="The one who rescued the infant Moses from the river and adopted him.",
 witness="Pharaoh's daughter was bathing at the river, saw the infant Moses among the reeds, and hearing him cry was moved with compassion: this is one of the Hebrews' children. Knowing her father the king had ordered the male infants killed, she yet adopted him, and even hired his own mother as nurse. A pagan princess's one thought of compassion preserved Israel's future deliverer.",
 follow=["Being moved with compassion and reaching out to the weak and helpless","Not fearing power, doing the good her conscience required"],
 caution=["Not letting 'the king's command' extinguish compassion"],
 applications=["Show mercy to the weak, abandoned lives near you","Before an unjust command, do good by conscience","Trust God can greatly use one small act of compassion"],
 prayer="Lord, You preserved Moses through Pharaoh's daughter's one thought of compassion. Make me keep compassion for the weak and helpless, not fearing power but doing good by conscience, a vessel in Your hand. Amen."),
"叶忒罗": dict(lesson="Wise advice is worth humbly receiving",
 summary="The priest of Midian, Moses' father-in-law, who gave Moses the wisdom to govern (also called Reuel).",
 witness="Jethro was the priest of Midian, Moses' father-in-law. When he saw Moses judging the people alone from morning to evening, he frankly said: this is not good; you will wear yourself out, and the people too; choose able, God-fearing men to govern at levels. Moses listened to his father-in-law and did so.",
 follow=["Giving the one in charge sound reminders with an outsider's clarity","Knowing to delegate at levels, not taking all the work on himself"],
 caution=["Don't, because you're the leader, become unable to hear counsel"],
 applications=["When ministry or work is overloaded, learn to choose trustworthy people to share it","Humbly receive reminders from those more experienced than you","Build reasonable division of labor for the team you lead"],
 prayer="Lord, Moses humbly received his father-in-law's advice. Make me not stubborn and self-willed, glad to take wise reminders, and know how to delegate and share, lest I exhaust body and soul. Amen."),
"革舜": dict(lesson="The sojourner's identity is also a reminder",
 summary="The firstborn son of Moses and Zipporah.",
 witness="Gershom was the firstborn son of Moses and Zipporah, his name meaning 'I have been a sojourner in a foreign land.' Moses commemorated his years sojourning in Midian with his son's name. This name also reminds God's people: we too are sojourners and pilgrims in the world, our true home is with God.",
 follow=["Remembering one's identity as a sojourner in the world"],
 caution=["Don't treat the place of sojourn as a permanent home"],
 applications=["Live in the world with the mindset of a pilgrim and sojourner","Don't be entangled by the things of this life; set your heart on the heavenly home","Still faithfully serve God in the situation of 'sojourning'"],
 prayer="Lord, Gershom's name reminds me I am a sojourner in the world. Make me live with a pilgrim's mindset, not coveting this life, setting my hope on Your eternal home. Amen."),
}
